# Deploying

A project made by [`howdy new`](/docs/cli/new) is ready to deploy as a
container image. It comes with a `Dockerfile` that builds one, and a
`bin/start` script that runs the app inside it. The app answers health
checks at `/up`, stops cleanly when it is told to, and migrates its
database as it starts.

This guide builds the image, runs it, and covers what every deploy needs to
get right: configuration, migrations, stopping, a shell on the running app,
and clusters. It ends with examples for Kamal, Kubernetes and a plain
server.

A project made with `--no-docker` has none of these files.
[`howdy add docker`](/docs/cli/add) adds them, and everything but the image
applies without them.

## Build the image

```sh
docker build -t my_app .
```

The `Dockerfile` builds in two stages:

1. The build stage copies Gleam's binary onto the official `erlang` Alpine
   image. It downloads the dependencies first, in a layer of their own, so
   a change to your code does not download them again. Then it runs
   `gleam export erlang-shipment`, which compiles the app and its
   dependencies and leaves out `dev/`, `test/` and the dev dependencies.
2. The image you run starts from the same `erlang` image, so compiled code
   and native drivers, such as SQLite's, match the Erlang they run on. It
   holds the shipment and `bin/start`, runs as an unprivileged user called
   `app`, and listens on `0.0.0.0:8787`.

The Gleam and Erlang/OTP versions are the ones installed where you ran
`howdy new`, set at the top of the `Dockerfile`:

```dockerfile
ARG GLEAM_VERSION=v1.19.2
ARG ERLANG_VERSION=29
```

Change them there when you upgrade either, so the image builds with what
you develop with. `--build-arg` overrides them for one build.

Build for the servers' architecture. From a Mac with Apple silicon to an
x86 server, that is `docker build --platform linux/amd64`.

`.dockerignore` keeps `build/`, `.env`, crash dumps and local SQLite
databases out of the build.

## Run it

With SQLite, the database lives in a volume at `/data`, so it outlives the
container:

```sh
docker run -d --name my_app -p 8787:8787 -v my_app_data:/data my_app
```

With PostgreSQL, give it the database's URL:

```sh
docker run -d --name my_app -p 8787:8787 \
  -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@db.internal:5432/my_app \
  my_app
```

To try the image beside the PostgreSQL from `docker-compose.yml`, run the
`app` profile:

```sh
docker compose --profile app up --build
```

Once the app is serving, `/up` answers and Docker reports the container as
healthy:

```sh
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/up
docker ps
```

The image's `HEALTHCHECK` asks `/up` every ten seconds. It allows thirty
seconds for the app to start before a failure counts.

## Configuration

The app reads its configuration from the environment as it starts, in
`main`. Set it on the platform:

- `HOST` and `PORT`: where the app listens. The image sets them to
  `0.0.0.0` and `8787`.
- `DATABASE_URL`, with PostgreSQL: the database to connect to.
- `DATABASE_PATH`, with SQLite: the database file. The image sets it to
  `/data/my_app.sqlite3`.
- `MIGRATE_ON_START`: `false` to leave migrations to a
  [step of their own](#Migrations).
- `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`: an OpenTelemetry collector to send traces
  to, in a project made without `--minimal`; see
  [Telemetry](/docs/telemetry/overview).
- `HOWDY_NODE_NAME`, `HOWDY_COOKIE` and `HOWDY_DIST_PORT`: Erlang
  distribution, for a [shell](#A-shell-on-the-running-app) or a
  [cluster](#Clusters).

Packages added with [`howdy add`](/docs/cli/add) read their own:

- auth: `APP_ORIGIN`, the site's public address, such as
  `https://my-app.example.com`. Sign-in links and cookies are made for it.
- mail: `MAIL_FROM`, and one of `SMTP_URL`, `RESEND_API_KEY` or
  `SENDGRID_API_KEY` to send through.
- remote: `RPC_TOKEN`, which callers over HTTP must send.

`.env.example` lists the ones a project uses. Do not ship a `.env` file:
values in it override the environment. `.dockerignore` keeps it out of the
image.

## Migrations

By default the app applies pending migrations as it starts, before it
serves a request:

- Migrations run in one transaction, holding a lock. Copies of the app that
  start together take turns, and the ones that come second find nothing
  left to do.
- A migration that fails stops the start. The new version never answers
  `/up`, so a platform that waits for it keeps the old version serving.

To migrate as a step of its own, before the new version starts, set
`MIGRATE_ON_START=false` on the app and run the image with `migrate`:

```sh
docker run --rm -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://... my_app migrate
docker run --rm -v my_app_data:/data my_app migrate
```

`migrate` applies what is pending, prints how many migrations it applied,
and exits. It exits with an error if one fails.

### Migrations the old version survives

Most deploys start the new version before stopping the old, so for a while
the old code runs against the new schema. Write migrations it can live
with:

- Add a column as nullable or with a default, so the old code's inserts
  still work.
- Add a column or table in one release, and use it in the next.
- Stop using a column in one release, and drop it in the next.
- Rename in steps: add the new column, write to both, move the data across,
  then drop the old one.

## Stopping

Platforms stop an app by sending it `SIGTERM`, then kill it if it is still
running after a while. A Howdy app stops cleanly in that time:

1. The server stops accepting connections.
2. Requests in flight get 8 seconds to finish, and WebSockets are closed
   with a going-away frame.
3. The rest of the app's supervision tree stops, and the VM exits.

The 8 seconds fit inside the 10 that `docker stop` waits. Change them with
`howdy.shutdown_timeout` in `configured`, in `src/my_app.gleam`, and keep
them below the platform's wait:

- `docker stop`: 10 seconds, or `stop_grace_period` in Docker Compose
- Kamal: `drain_timeout`, 30 seconds
- Kubernetes: `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, 30 seconds

[Supervision and shutdown](/docs/guides/supervision) explains how `main`
sets this up.

## A shell on the running app

`bin/start remote` opens an Erlang shell inside the running app. It
connects to the app over Erlang distribution, so the app must run as a
named node. Set `HOWDY_NODE_NAME` and `HOWDY_COOKIE`. To keep distribution
on the container's loopback, where only `remote` uses it, set these too:

```sh
docker run -d --name my_app -p 8787:8787 -v my_app_data:/data \
  -e HOWDY_NODE_NAME=my_app@localhost \
  -e HOWDY_COOKIE="$(openssl rand -hex 32)" \
  -e ERL_EPMD_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1 \
  -e ERL_FLAGS="-kernel inet_dist_use_interface {127,0,0,1}" \
  my_app
```

Then open the shell in the same container, where those variables are
already set:

```sh
docker exec -it my_app bin/start remote
```

It is an Erlang shell, not a Gleam one: GSH is a dev dependency, so it is
not in the image. Gleam modules are Erlang modules with `@` for `/`, and
strings are written `<<"like this">>`. The shortcuts in `src/console.gleam`
work as they do in the [console](/docs/guides/console):

```
(my_app@localhost)1> console:todos().
{ok,[{todo,1,<<"hello">>,false}]}
```

Everything you type runs in the app, with its database and its data.
Leave with `Ctrl+G` and then `q`. *Do not type `q().`*: in a remote shell it
stops the app.

`bin/start` writes the cookie to `~/.erlang.cookie`, where Erlang reads it,
rather than putting it on a command line where any process could see it.
Anyone with the cookie who can reach the node can run any code in it, so
keep the cookie secret.

## Clusters

Copies of the app on different servers can connect as an Erlang cluster.
[Howdy Remote](/docs/remote/clusters) calls procedures across it, and
channels and presence reach every node. Give each node a name the others
can reach it by, and the same cookie:

- `HOWDY_NODE_NAME`: the node's name, with a host the other nodes can
  reach, such as `my_app@10.0.0.5` or `my_app@app-1.internal`. A host with a
  dot gets a full name (`-name`) and one without a short name (`-sname`).
  An IPv6 address switches distribution to IPv6.
- `HOWDY_COOKIE`: the secret every node shares.
- `HOWDY_DIST_PORT`: the port the node listens on for other nodes, rather
  than a random one, so a firewall can allow it.

Other nodes find it through EPMD on port 4369, and then connect on the
distribution port. Open both between the servers, on a private network
only. Nodes do not find each other on their own: connect them with
`remote.connect_to`, which reconnects a node that drops.

Read [Distribution is full trust](/docs/remote/clusters) before you do.

Servers that share a PostgreSQL database need no cluster for
[notifications](/docs/notifications/overview#On-several-servers):
`notifications.across_servers` sends them to pages open on every server
through the database. Channels and presence still need the cluster.

## Kamal

[Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) deploys images to your own servers, behind
a proxy that waits for each new container to answer `/up` before sending
it traffic. That is the path the app answers, so a `config/deploy.yml` needs
little:

```yaml
service: my_app
image: my-user/my_app

servers:
  web:
    - 192.0.2.10

proxy:
  host: my-app.example.com
  ssl: true
  app_port: 8787

registry:
  username: my-user
  password:
    - KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD

builder:
  arch: amd64

env:
  clear:
    APP_ORIGIN: https://my-app.example.com
  secret:
    - DATABASE_URL
```

`.kamal/secrets` says where the secrets come from, such as
`DATABASE_URL=$DATABASE_URL`. Then `kamal setup` deploys for the first
time, and `kamal deploy` after that.

The new container migrates as it starts, and the proxy keeps sending
traffic to the old one until the new one is healthy. Kamal waits
`deploy_timeout`, 30 seconds by default, so raise it for migrations that
take longer. To migrate by hand, Kamal runs the image's commands:

```sh
kamal app exec migrate
kamal app exec -i --reuse "bin/start remote"
```

The first starts a container of its own; the second runs inside the
container that is serving, so it needs the node name and cookie in `env`.

With SQLite, keep the database in a volume and deploy to one server:

```yaml
volumes:
  - my_app_data:/data
```

During a deploy, the old and new containers use the database at the same
time for a few seconds. They share the server's disk, so SQLite's locks
keep them apart.

## Kubernetes

```yaml
spec:
  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30
  containers:
    - name: my-app
      image: registry.example.com/my_app:1.4.0
      ports:
        - containerPort: 8787
      env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: my-app
              key: database-url
      startupProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /up
          port: 8787
        failureThreshold: 30
        periodSeconds: 2
      livenessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /up
          port: 8787
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /up
          port: 8787
      lifecycle:
        preStop:
          exec:
            command: ["sleep", "5"]
```

- The startup probe gives the app a minute to migrate and start before the
  liveness probe can restart it.
- Kubernetes sends `SIGTERM` at about the time it stops routing to the
  pod, so requests can still arrive for a few seconds after. The `preStop`
  sleep lets those land before the server stops listening.
- For a readiness probe that also checks the database, mount a second
  health check at `/ready`; see
  [Health checks](/docs/guides/health-checks).
- To migrate before a rollout, set `MIGRATE_ON_START` to `false` and run
  the image as a `Job` with `args: ["migrate"]`.

## Without Docker

The shipment runs anywhere with the same Erlang/OTP version it was built
with. Build it, and put `bin/start` in its `bin/` folder:

```sh
gleam export erlang-shipment
mkdir -p build/erlang-shipment/bin
cp bin/start build/erlang-shipment/bin/
```

Copy `build/erlang-shipment` to the server, and run it with a service
manager. A `systemd` unit:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=my_app
After=network.target

[Service]
User=my_app
ExecStart=/srv/my_app/bin/start run
EnvironmentFile=/etc/my_app/env
Restart=on-failure
TimeoutStopSec=15

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

`systemd` stops the app with `SIGTERM`, as a container runtime does.
`/etc/my_app/env` holds the [configuration](#Configuration), one
`NAME=value` per line. Run `/srv/my_app/bin/start migrate` with the same
environment to migrate by hand.
